1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,500
 In this lesson, we'll go over one of the main use cases that we see developers using OpenAI functions for, tagging and extraction.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:14,380
 This allows us to extract structured data from unstructured text.

3
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:15,720
 Let's have some fun.

4
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:19,660
 The first use case that we'll cover is tagging.

5
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:25,800
 In tagging, we pass in an unstructured piece of text along with some structured description,

6
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:31,680
 and then we use the LLM to generate some structured output to reason over that input text

7
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:36,680
 and create some response in the format of the structured description that we pass in.

8
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:43,280
 So here in this example, we know that we want to be generating an object that has a sentiment of the text

9
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:46,060
 and also has a tag for the language of the text.

10
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:50,520
 And so when we pass in a piece of text, we'll pass in a structured description saying,

11
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:56,020
 hey, extract some sentiment, extract some language, and the LLM will reason over that text

12
00:00:56,020 --> 00:01:00,280
 and respond with an object that has a sentiment tag and a language tag.

13
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:05,240
 This is similar, but slightly different from the second use case that we'll cover, which is extraction.

14
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:11,480
 In extraction, we're going to be extracting specific entities from the text.

15
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:15,600
 These entities are also represented by a structured description.

16
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:19,880
 But rather than using the LLM to reason over the text

17
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:23,860
 and respond with a single output in this structured description,

18
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:29,640
 we're using the LLM to look over the text and extract a list of these elements.

19
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:32,520
 So we might ask it to look over an article

20
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:35,900
 and extract a list of the papers that were mentioned in that article.

21
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:38,400
 Let's take a look at doing this in code.

22
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:43,420
 First, we're going to import our standard setup.

23
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:50,180
 We're then going to import some of the functions and classes that we learned about in the last lesson.

24
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:53,420
 So we're going to import list from typing to help us with type hints.

25
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:55,860
 We're going to import base model and field from Pydantic.

26
00:01:55,860 --> 00:01:59,560
 And then we're going to import our convert Pydantic to OpenAI function.

27
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:05,460
 Let's now create a Pydantic model that we're going to use for tagging.

28
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:06,480
 So we're going to call it tagging.

29
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,340
 We're going to have a description where we just say tag the piece of text with particular info.

30
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:14,500
 And then we're going to have a list of the attributes that we want to tag the text with.

31
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:15,560
 So first, sentiment.

32
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:18,460
 And we're going to have the description be the sentiment of the text.

33
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:20,320
 It should be pause, neg, or neutral.

34
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,320
 So we're defining some values that the sentiment field should be.

35
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:25,120
 And remember, this gets passed into the language model.

36
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:29,940
 So this is how we're telling the language model what the shape of the data that we're extracting should be.

37
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:31,620
 And then we also have a language tag.

38
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:33,840
 And here we have the language of the text.

39
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:38,000
 And we're again saying it should be in this ISO 639-1 code.

40
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,880
 If we then call our convert Pydantic to OpenAI function method on this class,

41
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:51,420
 we can see that we get back a nice JSON blob with a lot of the name and description and parameters

42
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:54,340
 and properties that are necessary to pass into OpenAI functions.

43
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:57,880
 So let's now use this to actually do the tagging.

44
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:58,680
 What are we going to need?

45
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:00,040
 We're going to need two more things.

46
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:01,960
 We're going to need a prompt and a language model.

47
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:18,120
 So we're going to import a chat prompt template in our chat OpenAI model.

48
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:25,020
 We'll create our model really simply by setting temperature equals to zero.

49
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:29,040
 And we're going to do this because when we're tagging things, we generally want it to be pretty deterministic.

50
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:37,280
 We're then going to create our tagging functions, which are just going to be a single one at this point.

51
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:46,720
 And now we need the prompt that we're going to use to tell the language model how to do this.

52
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:48,620
 So we're going to have it be a chat prompt template.

53
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:53,780
 We're going to add in a pretty simple system message to start.

54
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:08,260
 And we can also use the system message in the future to convey more interesting and more detailed instructions.

55
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:15,980
 And then we'll add in a spot where we're going to pass in the user input.

56
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:21,420
 We're then going to create a model with functions.

57
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:27,700
 So we're going to bind the model to the tagging functions.

58
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:43,120
 And we're also going to add this function call parameter because we know that we're always going to do tagging.

59
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:47,740
 And so we're going to try to force it to always do this tagging function.

60
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:57,920
 We can then create a tagging chain by just combining the prompt with this model.

61
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:00,640
 And then we can call this.

62
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:10,600
 And then we can call this on a piece of text.

63
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:12,940
 And we can get back a response.

64
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:14,500
 And so we're calling the tagging function.

65
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:16,060
 We can see that the function call is there.

66
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:18,160
 And then we can see the arguments that are passed in.

67
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,560
 And we can see that sentiment is positive and language is English.

68
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:23,680
 We can do this for another piece of text.

69
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:26,620
 We can vary it up and try a different language and a different sentiment.

70
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:32,120
 So let's do some Italian.

71
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:40,080
 Which means I don't like this food.

72
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:41,760
 And so this should be negative.

73
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:43,120
 And it should be in Italian.

74
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:47,900
 So as you can see, we're getting the language model to respond in this format.

75
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:51,480
 But it's still kind of nested here in this additional Quargs bit.

76
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:53,740
 And we know that we're always going to be extracting the structure.

77
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:58,620
 And so what we really want to do is add an output parser that takes in this AI message

78
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:01,920
 and basically parses out the JSON and just says that.

79
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:03,540
 Because that's the only interesting thing here.

80
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:05,920
 We already know that we're going to be calling this function.

81
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:09,240
 So the fact that content is null, that's not interesting to us.

82
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:12,000
 The fact that there's a function calls made, that's not interesting to us.

83
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:12,800
 We're forcing it to do that.

84
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:16,160
 The fact that it's calling the tagging function, also not interesting to us.

85
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:18,960
 Because we know that it's going to be calling the tagging function because we forced it to.

86
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:22,980
 And so here what we really want is just the value of arguments, which is JSON blob.

87
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:27,660
 And it would be really convenient if that was parsed into JSON because it's JSON in this JSON blob.

88
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:30,000
 And we want to be able to use the individual elements.

89
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:34,640
 And so we have a nice little output parser in link chain that can help with exactly that.

90
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:40,640
 It's called JSON output functions parser.

91
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:45,200
 And so we'll import it from link chain output parsers, open AI functions.

92
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:48,780
 Then we can then recreate our tagging chain.

93
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:51,260
 And we can do this as the prompt.

94
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:54,160
 We can then combine it with the model with functions.

95
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:57,800
 And now we'll add in this output parser as the final element.

96
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:03,200
 Now, if we take the same thing, and we call it again,

97
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:08,760
 we can see that what we get back is just this dictionary, which has been parsed from the JSON blob.

98
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:12,140
 And so this is much more nice and convenient to work with downstream.

99
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:14,560
 Let's now go into extraction.

100
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:19,480
 An extraction is similar to a tagging, but we want to use it to extract multiple pieces of information.

101
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:24,820
 First, we'll define the piece of information that we want to extract.

102
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:25,980
 And this is a person schema.

103
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:28,300
 So we'll have information about a person.

104
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:30,660
 We'll have the name, which is a person's name.

105
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:33,820
 And then we'll have the age, which we'll mark as an optional integer.

106
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:39,500
 What we want to do is we want to extract a list of these objects.

107
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:44,100
 And so we'll define another class called information, which is just the information that we want to extract.

108
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:46,060
 We'll add an attribute, which is people.

109
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:50,100
 And then this will be a list of the person type.

110
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:55,460
 This information class is going to be the one that we're passing in as the OpenAI function.

111
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:01,140
 So here we can convert that class to OpenAI functions.

112
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:04,040
 And we can see that we have information properties.

113
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:06,220
 The main property is people.

114
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:12,020
 And then if we look at the description of people, we can see that we now have the person description here.

115
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:22,700
 So under the hood, in this convert pydantic to OpenAI function method, we take care of resolving any mentions and putting all the relevant information that's needed into this JSON blob.

116
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:25,800
 So now we're going to set up our extraction chain.

117
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:28,020
 First, we're going to define some extraction functions.

118
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:32,260
 We're going to call convert pydantic to OpenAI function on this information class.

119
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:34,660
 Next, we're going to set up the extraction model.

120
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:40,200
 So we're going to bind functions equal to extraction functions because we want to use those.

121
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:48,020
 And then we're going to bind function call with name set equal to information because the name of the function that we want to force the model to call is information.

122
00:08:48,020 --> 00:08:51,560
 After that, we can try it out.

123
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:55,540
 So let's try it out on a simple sentence.

124
00:08:55,540 --> 00:09:07,000
 And we can see that it extracts people as a list of one with name Joe, age 30.

125
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:07,940
 We can add in more.

126
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:10,760
 We can make it slightly more complex.

127
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:13,260
 His mom is Martha.

128
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:14,940
 So now there's two people here.

129
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:19,760
 And we can see that what it extracts is name Joe, age 30.

130
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:23,800
 Gets a second person this time, name Martha, age 0.

131
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:26,620
 So we don't actually mention Martha's aging here.

132
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:33,080
 And so the model seems to think that when it doesn't know a person's name, it should just put 0, which probably we can do better.

133
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:35,700
 We can force the model to respond in a more educated way.

134
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:40,980
 And so what we're going to do is we're going to add a prompt that's going to tell the language model to do that.

135
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:46,160
 So now we're adding a prompt and we have a system message where we say, extract the relevant information.

136
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:49,180
 If not explicitly provided, do not guess.

137
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:50,660
 Extract partial info.

138
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:59,180
 So hopefully what this should do is this should force the language model or this should allow the language model to not respond always with an age.

139
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:01,140
 It's not going to make up this age equals 0.

140
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:03,580
 So we can create a new extraction chain.

141
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:11,280
 As this prompt and then the extraction model.

142
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:24,460
 And then if we call this extraction chain on the same thing, we can now see that in the arguments we have name, Martha, and there's nothing mentioned about age.

143
00:10:24,460 --> 00:10:28,000
 So it's correctly reasoning that it doesn't need to pass in information about age.

144
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,240
 Again, we can probably do better than just this AI message.

145
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:33,860
 We can try to parse it into some structure.

146
00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:39,280
 So let's add back in that JSON output parser that we had before.

147
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:46,800
 If we call it again, we can see that it's parsed into this dictionary with people and then the list of names.

148
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:51,660
 For extraction, though, there's again some extraneous information here that we don't really need.

149
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:55,200
 We don't really care that there's this list of people here.

150
00:10:55,200 --> 00:11:03,360
 Because remember, when we defined it, information is just a vehicle to allow us to extract multiple elements of this person field.

151
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:06,640
 What we really care about is just this list of person.

152
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:09,840
 So we can use a different output parser for that.

153
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:13,780
 We can import JSON key output functions parser.

154
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:19,460
 And what this will basically do is it will look for a particular key in the output and extract only that.

155
00:11:19,460 --> 00:11:22,480
 So now we modify our extraction chain slightly.

156
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:26,820
 We pass in this new output parser.

157
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:30,460
 And we pass it in with a key name equal to people.

158
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:32,720
 Because this is the field that we want to extract.

159
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:39,260
 Now if we call this on the input again, we can see that now we just have this list.

160
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:45,620
 So it's a minor improvement, but it will make it easier to use downstream if extraction is truly what we care about for this purpose.

161
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:50,180
 Now is a good time to pause and play around with both tagging and extraction.

162
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:53,620
 Add some different models, try some different input text.

163
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:55,560
 There's a lot of cool things you can do here.

164
00:11:55,560 --> 00:12:00,120
 We're going to put this all together into a slightly more real world example.

165
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:07,100
 So what we're going to do is we're first going to load a real article from the internet.

166
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:11,480
 And so we're going to use a web-based loader, which comes from Langchain document loaders.

167
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:13,640
 And we cover what this is in a previous course.

168
00:12:13,640 --> 00:12:19,420
 And so we're going to pass in this URL here, which is a great blog post about autonomous agents.

169
00:12:19,420 --> 00:12:21,080
 We're going to call dot load.

170
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:23,100
 And this is going to load a few documents.

171
00:12:23,100 --> 00:12:25,060
 It's going to load one document in particular.

172
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:30,180
 And so we're going to create a single document object to represent that.

173
00:12:30,180 --> 00:12:35,180
 And then we're not going to print it all out because this is a rather large document.

174
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:44,780
 So what we're first going to do is we're going to get the first 10,000 characters of this.

175
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:51,440
 If we print out the first few bit of this page content, again, we're not going to print it all out because it's pretty long.

176
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:58,000
 But if we print out the beginning, we can see that it's an article about LLM-powered autonomous agents.

177
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:04,440
 And then we could probably do a little bit better cleanup of this, but we'll work with this text as is.

178
00:13:04,440 --> 00:13:09,260
 So we can see that it starts to finally get down into some introduction or some overview section.

179
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:13,600
 And so we're going to work with this text to do both tagging and extraction.

180
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:20,200
 First, we're going to create a class describing what we want to tag.

181
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:21,920
 So we want to get an overview.

182
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:23,960
 of this article.

183
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:28,400
 And so we want to extract a summary, the language that's used, and any keywords.

184
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:33,140
 And so we're going to create this nice model that we can use to describe all that.

185
00:13:33,140 --> 00:13:35,980
 We're then going to set up the chain.

186
00:13:35,980 --> 00:13:37,620
 And this is all very similar to above.

187
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:40,340
 So we're going to create the overview tagging functions.

188
00:13:40,340 --> 00:13:47,140
 And this is just converting one base model, the overview base model, to OpenAI functions using this method.

189
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:49,120
 We're then going to create our tagging model.

190
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:53,560
 So we're going to bind the functions that we created above and force it to call this overview.

191
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:56,140
 And then we're going to create a tagging chain.

192
00:13:56,140 --> 00:13:59,580
 So we're doing a prompt, which is the prompt that we used above.

193
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:03,160
 A tagging model, a tagging model, and then JSON output functions parser.

194
00:14:03,160 --> 00:14:09,760
 Putting that all together, we can now call tagging chain.invoke.

195
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:14,920
 and we can pass in the page content that we extracted above.

196
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:19,540
 And so we can see that it extracts a summary of the article.

197
00:14:19,540 --> 00:14:23,220
 This article discusses the concept of building autonomous agents powered by LLMs.

198
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:26,260
 It's in English and extracted a bunch of keywords.

199
00:14:26,260 --> 00:14:30,820
 LLM, autonomous agents, planning, memory, tool use, task, decomposition, self-reflection.

200
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:35,780
 Now we're going to try to extract all papers that are mentioned by this article.

201
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:39,760
 And this article is very good and very academic and so it mentions a lot of papers and we're

202
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:42,320
 really interested what the papers that it mentioned are.

203
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:45,140
 So we'll create some base models here.

204
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:46,040
 First up is paper.

205
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:49,960
 We want to know the title of the paper and then the author of the paper as well, if it's provided.

206
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:54,620
 And then we are going to put this inside another class called info and we're going to have papers

207
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:57,560
 be a list of paper so that we can get a lot of them back.

208
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:02,560
 We're then going to set up our extraction chain.

209
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:06,320
 And so here we're creating the functions that we want to pass in, which is just info.

210
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:09,200
 We're then binding that to the functions parameter.

211
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:12,280
 We're then binding function call and setting name equal to info.

212
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:15,420
 We're going to force it to call this info function.

213
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:20,400
 And then we're setting up our chain prompt into extraction model into this JSON key output

214
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,460
 functions parser.

215
00:15:21,460 --> 00:15:23,120
 And we're going to set key name equals to paper.

216
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:27,800
 So we're going to create that and then we can run it on the page content once again.

217
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:36,920
 And so we can see that we get one result back title, LLM Powered Autonomous Agents, author Lillian Wang.

218
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:44,600
 And so this is a little bit confusing because this is actually the title of the article that we're passing in

219
00:15:44,600 --> 00:15:46,520
 and the author of the article that we're passing in.

220
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:49,640
 It's not the papers that are mentioned within the article, it's the article itself.

221
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:54,920
 And so the language model is probably getting confused because if you remember the initial page is a lot of

222
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:57,240
 this is the article title, this is the author.

223
00:15:57,240 --> 00:16:03,000
 And we haven't really instructed the language model too clearly that it should be extracting rather than the

224
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:04,840
 information about the article itself.

225
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:07,320
 It should be extracting the papers that are mentioned within.

226
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:11,960
 And so in order to fix that, we're going to give it a slightly better system message.

227
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:17,320
 So we're going to tell it more explicitly, an article will be passed to you.

228
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:19,720
 Extract from it all papers that are mentioned by this article.

229
00:16:19,720 --> 00:16:22,040
 Do not extract the name of the article itself.

230
00:16:22,040 --> 00:16:23,800
 If no papers are mentioned, that's fine.

231
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:26,040
 You don't need to extract any, just return an empty list.

232
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:29,400
 Do not make up or guess any extra information.

233
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:32,040
 Only extract exactly what is in the text.

234
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:36,600
 And so we're going to create this prompt that is much more descriptive in how the language model

235
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:37,400
 should behave.

236
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:40,040
 We're going to use this prompt in our new chain.

237
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:41,080
 Other than that, it's the same.

238
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:44,120
 So it's got the same extraction model, the same output parser.

239
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:49,560
 And then we're going to call this new chain on that page content.

240
00:16:49,560 --> 00:16:54,840
 And we're going to get back a list of articles with titles and authors.

241
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:57,400
 And these look much more reasonable.

242
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:02,760
 And so these are all great papers in this space that are not the article itself,

243
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:04,920
 but rather the author is using to make some points.

244
00:17:04,920 --> 00:17:09,160
 We can also do some sanity checks to make sure that it's performing okay.

245
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:13,000
 So we can pass in a simple message like, hi, we'd expect this to return an empty list.

246
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:13,720
 And indeed it does.

247
00:17:13,720 --> 00:17:18,360
 And so here are instructions about just returning an empty list.

248
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:21,880
 If there's no papers that are mentioned, they're working okay.

249
00:17:21,880 --> 00:17:24,440
 So it seems to be doing a reasonable pass here.

250
00:17:24,440 --> 00:17:28,920
 Remember though, this is just the first 10,000 characters of the article.

251
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:30,440
 What if we want to do it on the whole article

252
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:32,920
 and extract all the papers that are mentioned in the whole article?

253
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:38,360
 In order to do that, we'll use some more concepts this time around text splitting.

254
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:39,800
 So we're going to define a text splitter.

255
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:42,760
 And we're going to use the recursive character text splitter.

256
00:17:42,760 --> 00:17:46,600
 We covered this in the previous course on deep learning.

257
00:17:47,160 --> 00:17:49,560
 So if this is unfamiliar, I would check that out.

258
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:52,360
 And the reason that we need to do this text splitting is that this article is really,

259
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:52,840
 really long.

260
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:55,960
 And so if we try to pass that article to the language model directly,

261
00:17:55,960 --> 00:17:58,600
 it's actually going to be too big for the token window.

262
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:02,440
 And so what we're going to do is we're going to split it into smaller pieces of text.

263
00:18:02,440 --> 00:18:04,680
 And then we're going to pass those pieces of text

264
00:18:04,680 --> 00:18:06,600
 to the language model individually.

265
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:08,920
 And then we're going to combine all the results at the end.

266
00:18:09,640 --> 00:18:14,120
 So let's create some splits by calling split text on the page content of the document.

267
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:20,920
 And if we look at how many splits we have, we can see that we have 14 different splits.

268
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:23,960
 And so what we're going to try to do now is we're going to create an entire

269
00:18:23,960 --> 00:18:27,960
 chain using link chain expression language that does everything that I just said.

270
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:29,480
 We're going to take in this page content.

271
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:32,680
 We're then going to split it up into splits.

272
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:35,800
 We're then going to pass all those individual splits

273
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:38,840
 to the extraction chain that we've defined above.

274
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:41,240
 And then we're going to join all the results together.

275
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:44,440
 And so one thing that we'll definitely need to do is we'll need to create

276
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:47,160
 a function that can join lists of lists.

277
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:50,520
 And so we're going to create this flatten function here.

278
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:53,400
 And this just very simply takes in a list of lists and flattens them.

279
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:59,160
 So if we do flatten on, let's do a list of 1, 2.

280
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:03,320
 And then we have a second element, which is a list of 3, 4.

281
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:06,120
 And we call flatten and it returns a flattened list, 1, 2, 3, 4.

282
00:19:06,120 --> 00:19:09,720
 And this is useful because we're going to extract a list of papers mentioned

283
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:11,960
 for each split and then merge them all together.

284
00:19:11,960 --> 00:19:17,080
 The other thing that we're going to need to do is have some method of preparing

285
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:19,240
 the splits to be passed into the chain.

286
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:23,320
 So remember, the chain takes in an input variable.

287
00:19:23,320 --> 00:19:25,960
 And specifically, you need to use a dictionary with an input key.

288
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:30,360
 This list of splits, if we look at the first one,

289
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:37,320
 it's just text.

290
00:19:37,880 --> 00:19:42,200
 So we're going to need some way of taking this list of texts and converting it into

291
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:46,040
 a list of dictionaries where that text is now the input key.

292
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:50,920
 We're going to do that by defining a function for that because this is going to be the first

293
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:52,840
 function that happens in the chain.

294
00:19:52,840 --> 00:19:55,320
 We're going to wrap it in a runnable lambda.

295
00:19:55,320 --> 00:20:01,560
 So a runnable lambda is just a simple wrapper in LangChain that takes in a function or a lambda

296
00:20:01,560 --> 00:20:03,800
 and then converts it into this runnable object.

297
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:07,640
 This is necessary to do when you have functions that are the first element in the chain so that

298
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:16,120
 they can be successfully piped together.

299
00:20:16,120 --> 00:20:23,080
 So we're going to define this preprocessing function here.

300
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:30,280
 So this is constructing a lambda where it takes as input and we want to pass the document as input

301
00:20:30,280 --> 00:20:32,600
 or rather the page content of the document as input.

302
00:20:32,600 --> 00:20:37,320
 So the X here is going to be a string and what we're doing is we're creating a function that takes

303
00:20:37,320 --> 00:20:44,040
 that string, splits and constructs a list of dictionaries where each dictionary is an input

304
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:45,800
 corresponding to the split.

305
00:20:47,080 --> 00:20:53,640
 If we play around with this and see what it does, we can call it on a string and we get back

306
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:55,640
 a list of dictionaries.

307
00:20:55,640 --> 00:20:59,640
 Here there's just one dictionary because the text splitter doesn't split it up because this text

308
00:20:59,640 --> 00:21:00,680
 is very short.

309
00:21:00,680 --> 00:21:06,840
 But basically it's taking in this string, splitting it up and creating a list of dictionaries.

310
00:21:06,840 --> 00:21:11,080
 And the reason that this is necessary is because this is going to be the input to the next part,

311
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:11,880
 the extraction chain.

312
00:21:11,880 --> 00:21:14,200
 And so we want to create a bunch of inputs there.

313
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:16,920
 So now we can create our chain.

314
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:21,000
 So we're going to have this prep function.

315
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,880
 We then want to pass this to the extraction chain.

316
00:21:23,880 --> 00:21:28,120
 But remember the extraction chain operates over a single element.

317
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:31,080
 And here we have a list of elements that we want to pass in.

318
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:34,680
 So what we can do is we can call dot map on the extraction chain.

319
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:39,640
 And this is basically saying take the previous input, which here is going to be a list of elements

320
00:21:39,640 --> 00:21:42,120
 and map this chain over them.

321
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:45,560
 And so that's going to lead to a list of lists.

322
00:21:45,560 --> 00:21:49,400
 Because again, this extraction chain is returning a list as we defined it.

323
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:52,600
 And so we're going to call flatten on it and fly in here.

324
00:21:52,600 --> 00:21:55,320
 This can just be the normal function that we defined above.

325
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:59,000
 We don't need to wrap it in a runnable lambda because it's not the first one in the sequence.

326
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,160
 We could if we wanted to, but it's not necessary.

327
00:22:01,160 --> 00:22:03,640
 So here we have this chain.

328
00:22:03,640 --> 00:22:09,480
 And if we call chain.invoke and we'll pass in the whole page content of the document,

329
00:22:09,480 --> 00:22:13,720
 we can see that it will take a little bit, but it will respond with the answer.

330
00:22:13,720 --> 00:22:17,000
 And so what it's doing is first it's splitting it into 14 sections.

331
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,240
 And it's then passing that to the extraction chain.

332
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:21,800
 When it passes it to the extraction chain,

333
00:22:21,800 --> 00:22:25,160
 it's actually paralyzing a lot of those calls automatically.

334
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:28,680
 The default that it paralyzes by is five calls.

335
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:32,360
 So it's not fully paralyzing, but it's speeding up a significant amount.

336
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:37,560
 When all of those calls are done, it will then get passed into the final flatten function.

337
00:22:37,560 --> 00:22:39,560
 And there we go.

338
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:43,400
 It returns a list of papers that it extracted.

339
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:45,560
 So it's got the title and it's got the author.

340
00:22:45,560 --> 00:22:50,280
 If we skim through, we can see that it's leaving the author empty for some of them.

341
00:22:50,280 --> 00:22:52,520
 If we really wanted to tell it to just omit that completely,

342
00:22:52,520 --> 00:22:54,440
 we could go back and change the prompt.

343
00:22:54,440 --> 00:22:56,280
 We can scroll down.

344
00:22:57,160 --> 00:23:00,520
 We can see here that it seems to be making some up.

345
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:02,760
 So there's paper A, author A.

346
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:04,200
 This appears to be incorrect.

347
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:07,880
 But if you actually look at the article that this is referencing,

348
00:23:07,880 --> 00:23:13,480
 this article is itself an article about prompting and covers, among other things,

349
00:23:13,480 --> 00:23:17,960
 extraction and retrieval augmented generation and citing things.

350
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:22,520
 And so there's actually a bunch of language in there that's imitating some fake papers

351
00:23:22,520 --> 00:23:24,120
 and having the response cite those.

352
00:23:24,120 --> 00:23:26,200
 And so it's actually picking this up correctly.

353
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:30,440
 One really interesting thing is that when you do extraction or even when you do question answering

354
00:23:30,440 --> 00:23:35,400
 over articles that talk about prompting or talk about language models and have examples of prompts in

355
00:23:35,400 --> 00:23:39,160
 there, sometimes the language model can get confused and mess things up.

356
00:23:39,160 --> 00:23:40,120
 But that's an aside.

357
00:23:40,120 --> 00:23:43,560
 That wraps up the end-to-end example here.

358
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:47,000
 This is a good time to pause and try it out on other real world examples.

359
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:50,840
 Try it out on a PDF and extract some information from that.

360
00:23:50,840 --> 00:23:53,000
 Try it out on another web page.

361
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:55,080
 Mess around with what you want to extract.

362
00:23:55,080 --> 00:24:01,240
 Tagging and extraction are some of the most popular use cases for this structured data extraction.

363
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:04,120
 And so gaining familiarity with this will go a long way.

364
00:24:04,120 --> 00:24:07,720
 In the next class, we'll talk about something slightly different.

365
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:13,240
 We'll talk about using OpenAI functions, not for extraction, but for deciding what functions to call.

366
00:24:13,240 --> 00:24:14,120
See you there.

